  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nWhile mid-level clouds have been encroaching on our area from SD, \nlow-level dry air has largely kept any rain from reaching the \nsurface. Virga will be expected this afternoon and evening as the \nclouds move east across MN/WI, but did keep 10% PoPs in the forecast \nin case of a sprinkle. Lows tonight will not be nearly as cool as \nlast night, owing to the greater cloud cover and an incoming warmer \nair mass ahead of a shortwave. 40s are expected for most places \nexcept for our far eastern WI counties where mid to upper 30s are \nmore likely. Highs Saturday will be a few degrees warmer than today, \nthough cloud cover will increase again in the afternoon as the \naforementioned shortwave approaches. CAMs favor an area of showers \ndeveloping from central to central to southern MN during the early \nto mid afternoon, which then travels east into WI by evening. \nRainfall amounts currently don't look to be too heavy, though 0.1-\n0.2\" is a possibility. A localized area could potentially receive \nmore if a stray thunderstorm occurs. If you have outdoor activities \nplanned on Saturday, it would be a good idea to have an umbrella or \nshelter to dodge the rain. Winds will also be breezy Saturday \nafternoon out of the south as a LLJ advances ahead of the mid-level \ntrough. Gusts will generally reach 25-30 mph across MN with isolated \ngusts of 35+ mph possible near the Buffalo Ridge. The winds will \nturn northwesterly and slow as we go into Saturday night.\n\nAfter the rain and shortwave moves east during Saturday night, the \nforecast looks very quiet for most of next week. Long-range \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance shows the upper-level jetstream \nremaining mainly off to our north. This should allow any shortwaves \nand their forcing to pass well away from our CWA, greatly limiting \nour chances for precipitation. Temperatures will slightly cool \nSunday and Monday following a cold frontal passage Saturday night. \nHighs during this time are forecast to be in the low to mid 60s. The \nbest chance for frost in the forecast period will be Sunday night \nwhen lows could dip into the mid 30s in eastern MN and northwest WI. \nAfterwards, temperatures will warm to above normal (highs in the mid \n60s to lower 70s) through next Friday as broad ridging builds over \nthe western half of the CONUS. The first full week of October looks \ngreat if you have any outdoor activities planned!",
